lesson: 253
title: Laying the table
level: primary-3
stars: 0
explain: When a table is laid, every person gets a knife, a fork and a spoon: 3 pieces of cutlery for each person.
explain: First count all the people at the table: the family and the guests together.
explain: The 4 of us and 3 guests: 4 + 3 = 7 people, and 7 × 3 = 21 knives, forks and spoons.
vars: a=name b=name f=2..6 g=2..9
answer: (f + g) * 3
exercise: {a}'s family is {f} people, and {g} guests are coming to dinner. How many knives, forks and spoons does {a} lay on the table?
exercise: {a} lays the table for {f} family members and {g} visitors. How many pieces of cutlery does {a.he} put out?
exercise: There are {f} people in {a}'s house, and {g} friends come for lunch. How many knives, forks and spoons are needed?
exercise: For Sunday dinner, {f} family members and {g} guests sit down. How many pieces of cutlery are on the table?
exercise: {a} and {b} set the table for their {f} family members and {g} neighbours. How many knives, forks and spoons do they lay?
exercise: lay the table for the {f} of us n {g} guests. how many knives forks n spoons
exercise: we're {f} plus {g+1}, no wait {g} people coming. how much cutlery do i put out
exercise: mum says set the table, {f} of us and {g} rellies coming, oh and use the nice napkins. knives forks spoons how many
exercise: {g} mates coming for tea + the {f} of us. how many bits of cutlery
exercise: y'all set for dinner? {f} family {g} guests, how many knives forks and spoons
test: How many pieces of cutlery lie on a table laid for {g} guests and the {f} hosts?
test: Dinner is for {f} people who live in the house and {g} who are visiting. Count the knives, forks and spoons laid.
test: {a} puts out cutlery for the {g} guests first and then for the {f} people of the family. How many knives, forks and spoons are on the table?
test: {f} of us, {g} guests. cutlery total?
test: {a} reckons {f+g} knives forks n spoons is enough for {f} of us and {g} guests. how many we actually need
test: {g} guests, {f} fam, {b} brought dessert. how many knives forks spoons on the table
exam: It is given that a household of {f.words} persons is to entertain {g.words} guests at dinner. Determine the aggregate number of knives, forks and spoons to be laid.
exam: A table is to be laid for {f} residents and {g} visitors. Ascertain the total quantity of items of cutlery requisite for this purpose.
exam: Compute the number of knives, forks and spoons necessitated by a luncheon attended by {g} guests in addition to {f} members of the host family.
exam: Whereas {f} family members and {g} invitees are to be seated at table, state the cardinality of the set of knives, forks and spoons required.
exercise: Grandma passed away, and after the funeral {g} relatives come back for dinner with {a}'s family of {f}. How many knives, forks and spoons does {a} lay on the table?
exercise: dad's between jobs so we're all eating in tonight, {f} of us and {g} freeloading cousins. knife, fork, spoon each, how many bits of cutlery, idiot?
test: {a}'s family used to be {f+1}, but Grandpa passed away in May. Tonight the {f} of them eat with {g} guests, and every person gets a knife, a fork and a spoon. How much cutlery is on the table?
